= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**No.** Take $P=2$, $Q=9$. The <Jacobi symbol> is
$$
\left(\frac29\right)=\left(\frac23\right)^2=(-1)^2=1,
$$
but the squares modulo $3$ are $0,1$. A square root of $2$ modulo $9$ would reduce to one modulo $3$, which is impossible. A <Jacobi symbol> of $1$ records a product of local signs; it does not assert that every local sign is positive, or that a <quadratic congruence> is soluble.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Factor $Q=\prod_i p_i$ and $Q'=\prod_j q_j$, retaining repeated <prime factors>. If $P$ has a common <prime factor> with $QQ'$, it has one with at least one of $Q,Q'$, and both sides of the desired equality are zero. Otherwise the definition of the <Jacobi symbol> gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac P{QQ'}\right)=\prod_i\left(\frac P{p_i}\right)\prod_j\left(\frac P{q_j}\right)=\left(\frac P Q\right)\left(\frac P{Q'}\right).}
$$
An empty product is $1$, so the argument includes either denominator being $1$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

For a <prime number> $p$, <multiplicativity of the Legendre symbol> says $(PP'/p)=(P/p)(P'/p)$, including the case of a zero factor. Therefore, writing $Q=\prod_i p_i$ with repetitions,
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{PP'}Q\right)=\prod_i\left(\frac{PP'}{p_i}\right)=\prod_i\left(\frac P{p_i}\right)\prod_i\left(\frac{P'}{p_i}\right)=\left(\frac P Q\right)\left(\frac{P'}Q\right).}
$$
This product definition still agrees with the stipulated zero when the numerator and denominator are not <coprime>. For $Q=1$ all three symbols are $1$.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

If $P\equiv P'\pmod Q$, then $P\equiv P'\pmod p$ for each <prime factor> $p$ of $Q$. The <Legendre symbol> depends only on the numerator's <residue class>, so the corresponding factors agree. Also $\gcd(P,Q)=\gcd(P',Q)$, covering the zero cases. Multiplying gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac P Q\right)=\left(\frac{P'}Q\right).}
$$
The convention for denominator $1$ gives the same conclusion there.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The supplementary law for the <Legendre symbol> is $(-1/p)=(-1)^{(p-1)/2}$ for odd <prime numbers> $p$. For odd integers $a,b$,
$$
\frac{ab-1}{2}=\frac{a-1}{2}+\frac{b-1}{2}+\frac{(a-1)(b-1)}2,
$$
and the last term is even. <Induction> over the <prime factors> of $Q$ therefore gives $\sum_i(p_i-1)/2\equiv(Q-1)/2\pmod2$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{-1}Q\right)=\prod_i(-1)^{(p_i-1)/2}=(-1)^{(Q-1)/2}.}
$$
For $Q=1$ the exponent is zero.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The second supplementary law for the <Legendre symbol> is $(2/p)=(-1)^{(p^2-1)/8}$. To multiply these signs, observe that for odd $a,b$,
$$
\frac{a^2b^2-1}{8}-\frac{a^2-1}{8}-\frac{b^2-1}{8}=\frac{(a^2-1)(b^2-1)}8.
$$
Both factors in the numerator are divisible by $8$, so the right side is even. Applying this identity repeatedly to the <prime factorization> gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac2Q\right)=\prod_i(-1)^{(p_i^2-1)/8}=(-1)^{(Q^2-1)/8}.}
$$
Again the convention at $Q=1$ agrees.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Write $P=\prod_i p_i$, $Q=\prod_j q_j$, counting repetitions. Since $P,Q$ are <coprime>, every pair $p_i,q_j$ consists of distinct odd <prime numbers>. <Quadratic reciprocity> for the <Legendre symbol> gives
$$
\left(\frac{p_i}{q_j}\right)=(-1)^{((p_i-1)/2)((q_j-1)/2)}\left(\frac{q_j}{p_i}\right).
$$
Multiplying over all $i,j$ and using <multiplicativity of the Jacobi symbol> yields the sign exponent
$$
\left(\sum_i\frac{p_i-1}{2}\right)\left(\sum_j\frac{q_j-1}{2}\right)\equiv\frac{P-1}{2}\frac{Q-1}{2}\pmod2,
$$
by the odd-product parity identity proved in (b)(i). Thus the <Jacobi reciprocity law> is
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac P Q\right)=(-1)^{(P-1)(Q-1)/4}\left(\frac Q P\right).}
$$
If either integer is $1$, its factorization is empty and both sides equal $1$.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

Complete the square to obtain the equivalent <quadratic congruence>
$$
(x+3)^2\equiv1111\pmod{8093}.
$$
Here $\gcd(1111,8093)=1$: the <Euclidean algorithm> has successive nonzero remainders $316,163,153,10,3,1$. If this <modular congruence> had a solution, the <Jacobi symbol> $(1111/8093)$ would be $1$, since a square <coprime> to an odd modulus has <Legendre symbol> $1$ at every <prime factor>. Calculate instead, using <Jacobi reciprocity> and reduction of numerators,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(\frac{1111}{8093}\right)
&=\left(\frac{8093}{1111}\right)&& (8093\equiv1\pmod4)\\
&=\left(\frac{316}{1111}\right)&& (8093=7\cdot1111+316)\\
&=\left(\frac4{1111}\right)\left(\frac{79}{1111}\right)=\left(\frac{79}{1111}\right)\\
&=-\left(\frac{1111}{79}\right)&& (79\equiv1111\equiv3\pmod4)\\
&=-\left(\frac5{79}\right)&& (1111=14\cdot79+5)\\
&=-\left(\frac{79}5\right)=-\left(\frac45\right)=-1.
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore **there is no solution**. This obstruction does not require a separate primality test for $8093$; the <Jacobi symbol> suffices.

= 2
{parent=2021}
{scope}
{title2=Group theory}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Let $|G|=p^2$. The allowed <center of a group> theorem gives $|Z(G)|>1$. By <Lagrange's theorem> its order is $p$ or $p^2$. In the first case $G/Z(G)$ has order $p$ and is a <cyclic group>. A <cyclic quotient by the center> forces $G$ to be <abelian>: if $G/Z(G)=\langle gZ(G)\rangle$, write arbitrary elements as $g^az,g^bw$ with $z,w\in Z(G)$, and commute them directly. Hence in every case
$$
\boxed{|G|=p^2\ \Longrightarrow\ G\text{ is abelian}.}
$$
For two examples at prime-cube order, take the <dihedral group> of a square, $\langle r,s\mid r^4=s^2=1,\ srs=r^{-1}\rangle$, and the <quaternion group> $Q_8=\{\pm1,\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}$. Both have order $8=2^3$. They are nonabelian because $sr\ne rs$ and $ij=k\ne-k=ji$, respectively. They are distinct examples even up to <group isomorphism>: the <dihedral group> has five elements of order two and the <quaternion group> has only one. The request asks for two examples; it does not require the prime to be arbitrary.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Write $|G|=pq$ with $p<q$. The <Sylow theorems> give $n_q\mid p$ and $n_q\equiv1\pmod q$. Since $p<q$, necessarily $n_q=1$. Its unique <Sylow subgroup> $N$ is a <normal subgroup> and has prime order $q$, so
$$
\boxed{N\cong C_q\trianglelefteq G.}
$$
A <Sylow subgroup> $H\cong C_p$ has trivial intersection with $N$, and $|NH|=|N||H|=pq$, so $NH=G$. Thus
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_q\rtimes C_p.}
$$
The <semidirect product> action is a <group homomorphism> $C_p\to\operatorname{Aut}(C_q)\cong C_{q-1}$. If the action is trivial, $G\cong C_{pq}$ by the <Chinese remainder theorem>; if it is nontrivial, $p\mid q-1$. In particular $G$ has both a cyclic normal subgroup and a cyclic quotient, making it a <metacyclic group> and hence a <solvable group>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The <Abelian group criterion for a product of two prime squares> applies because $11<19$ and $11\nmid19^2-1=360$. Here its <Sylow theorems> proof is especially short:
$$
n_{19}\mid121,\quad n_{19}\equiv1\pmod{19};\qquad n_{11}\mid361,\quad n_{11}\equiv1\pmod{11}.
$$
The candidates for $n_{19}$ are $1,11,121$, of which only $1$ has the required remainder. The candidates for $n_{11}$ are $1,19,361$, again leaving only $1$. Thus both <Sylow subgroups> $P,Q$ are <normal subgroups>. Their <group commutators> lie in $P\cap Q=\{1\}$, and $PQ=G$, so $G\cong P\times Q$. Both factors are <abelian groups of prime-square order> by (a).

The <fundamental theorem of finitely generated abelian groups> gives precisely
$$
\boxed{\begin{gathered}
C_{121}\times C_{361},\qquad C_{121}\times C_{19}\times C_{19},\\
C_{11}\times C_{11}\times C_{361},\qquad C_{11}\times C_{11}\times C_{19}\times C_{19}.
\end{gathered}}
$$
These four <groups> are pairwise nonisomorphic: their characteristic <Sylow subgroups> differ in whether they contain an element of order $11^2$ or $19^2$. The first is also the <cyclic group> $C_{43681}$.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

Apply the <Abelian group criterion for a product of two prime squares> with the smaller and larger primes in their correct order. For $p<11$, all such <groups> are <abelian> exactly when $p\nmid11^2-1=120$. Of the primes $2,3,5,7$, only $7$ qualifies. For $p>11$, the criterion is $11\nmid p^2-1$, equivalently $p\not\equiv\pm1\pmod{11}$. Therefore the exact answer is
$$
\boxed{p=7\quad\text{or}\quad p>11\text{ prime with }p\not\equiv1,10\pmod{11}.}
$$
Equivalently, among the stipulated primes $p\ne11$, exclude $2,3,5$ and the residue classes $\pm1$ modulo $11$.

For completeness, the necessity in that criterion supplies actual counterexamples at every excluded prime. If $\ell<q$ and $\ell\mid q^2-1$, the <multiplicative group of a finite field is cyclic>, so choose $\alpha\in\mathbb F_{q^2}^{\times}$ of order $\ell$. Let a generator of $C_{\ell^2}$ act on the additive <group> of $\mathbb F_{q^2}$ by multiplication by $\alpha$. This defines a nontrivial <group action>, hence a nonabelian <semidirect product> of order $\ell^2q^2$. Use $(\ell,q)=(p,11)$ for $p=2,3,5$, and $(\ell,q)=(11,p)$ for $p\equiv\pm1\pmod{11}$ with $p>11$. Thus no excluded prime can be restored to the answer.

= e
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}
{c}

Take **$p=5$**. The unit $3$ modulo $121$ has order $5$, because
$$
3^5=243\equiv1\pmod{121},\qquad3\not\equiv1\pmod{121}.
$$
Let a generator $b$ of $C_{25}$ act on $C_{121}=\langle a\rangle$ by $a\mapsto a^3$. The action is well defined since its order $5$ divides $25$. The <semidirect product>
$$
\boxed{G=C_{121}\rtimes C_{25}=\langle a,b\mid a^{121}=b^{25}=1,\ bab^{-1}=a^3\rangle}
$$
has order $121\cdot25=11^2 5^2$. Its construction as a <semidirect product> ensures that $a$ retains order $121$. Since $a^3\ne a$, the <groups> generated by $a$ and $b$ do not commute, so $G$ is nonabelian.

