{title2=Projective geometry}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A <projective transformation> of $\mathbb P(\mathbb F^{n+1})$ is induced by an invertible <linear map> $A:\mathbb F^{n+1}\to\mathbb F^{n+1}$:
$$
\boxed{\tau([v])=[Av],\qquad A\in\operatorname{GL}_{n+1}(\mathbb F).}
$$
This is well defined because replacing $v$ by a nonzero scalar multiple does not change $[Av]$. Multiplying $A$ by a nonzero scalar also leaves $\tau$ unchanged. Conversely these are precisely the transformations in the <projective linear group> $\operatorname{PGL}_{n+1}(\mathbb F)$. No field automorphism is included in this definition.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Choose nonzero vectors $p_i\in\mathbb F^2$ representing $P_i$, and write $[u,v]=\det(u,v)$. Use the <cross-ratio> convention
$$
\boxed{(P_0P_1:P_2P_3)=\frac{[p_0,p_2][p_1,p_3]}{[p_0,p_3][p_1,p_2]}.}
$$
Distinctness makes the denominator nonzero. Every rescaling of a representative occurs once in numerator and denominator, so the value depends only on the <projective points>. Swapping both pairs simply exchanges the numerator's factors and the denominator's factors:
$$
(P_1P_0:P_3P_2)=\frac{[p_1,p_3][p_0,p_2]}{[p_1,p_2][p_0,p_3]}=(P_0P_1:P_2P_3).
$$
Under an invertible <linear map> $A$, $[Au,Av]=\det(A)[u,v]$. Two factors of $\det(A)$ occur above and below the fraction, proving <projective transformation> invariance.

Finally choose <homogeneous coordinates> making $P_0=[1:0]$, $P_1=[0:1]$, $P_2=[1:1]$. This normalization exists by the allowed theorem about a <projective frame>. A point distinct from $P_0$ is uniquely $[t:1]$, and the displayed <cross-ratio> is $t$. Thus equality of the two <cross-ratios> forces equal coordinates and
$$
\boxed{P_3=Q.}
$$
This also shows that for fixed first three distinct points the fourth point is determined by its <cross-ratio>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Conjugate by a <projective transformation> sending $P$ to $[1:0]$ and $Q$ to $[0:1]$. A matrix representing the conjugated transformation must have zero diagonal, since its first column represents $[0:1]$ and its second represents $[1:0]$. Therefore it has the form
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}0&a\\b&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad ab\ne0.
$$
Its square is $abI$, a nonzero scalar matrix, which induces the identity on the <projective line>. Conjugating back proves
$$
\boxed{\tau^2=\operatorname{id}.}
$$
For the requested explicit <involution>, take
$$
\boxed{\tau([x:y])=[y:x].}
$$
More generally $[x:y]\mapsto[ay:bx]$ works whenever $ab\ne0$.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The four points form a <projective frame>: no three are collinear. By the allowed uniqueness theorem and <cross-ratio> invariance, use
$$
P_0=[1:0:0],\quad P_1=[0:1:0],\quad P_2=[0:0:1],\quad P_3=[1:1:1].
$$
Intersecting the indicated <projective lines> gives
$$
Q_0=[1:0:1],\qquad Q_1=[1:1:0],\qquad Q_2=[0:1:1].
$$
A point of $Q_0Q_2$ has coordinates $[a:b:a+b]$. Intersecting with $P_0P_1$, whose equation is $z=0$, gives $Q_3=[1:-1:0]$. On that line use its first two <homogeneous coordinates>. The <cross-ratio> from (a)(ii) is
$$
\boxed{(P_0P_1:Q_3Q_1)=\frac{(-1)(-1)}{(1)(-1)}=-1.}
$$
The <characteristic of a field> being zero ensures that $Q_3\ne Q_1$. The construction is the diagonal-point property of a <complete quadrangle>, producing <harmonic conjugate points>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Choose a point $A$ off $L$, then a point $B$ on $AR_2$ distinct from $A,R_2$. Put
$$
P_0=R_0,\quad P_1=R_1,\quad P_2=A,\quad P_3=B.
$$
These four points form a <projective frame>: $A,B$ lie off $L$, and $AB=AR_2$ meets $L$ at neither $R_0$ nor $R_1$. Form, using only joins and intersections,
$$
U=R_0A\cap R_1B,\qquad V=R_0B\cap R_1A,\qquad \boxed{R_3=UV\cap L.}
$$
Here the point called $Q_1$ in (i) is $R_2$, while $Q_3$ is $R_3$. Thus (i) gives $(R_0R_1:R_3R_2)=-1$. Interchanging the last two arguments reciprocates the <cross-ratio>, so
$$
\boxed{(R_0R_1:R_2R_3)=(-1)^{-1}=-1.}
$$
All auxiliary choices are possible over a <field> of characteristic zero, which is infinite. Uniqueness from (a)(ii) shows that the final point is independent of those choices. The picture uses an affine chart in which every relevant intersection is finite; parallel-line exceptions in another chart are ordinary intersections on the <projective line at infinity>.

