{scope}
{title2=Introduction to manifolds}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Take $\Omega=\mathbb R^2$ and
$$
\boxed{F(s,t)=(e^s\cos t,e^s\sin t).}
$$
Its <Jacobian matrix> and <determinant> are
$$
DF_{(s,t)}=e^s\begin{pmatrix}\cos t&-\sin t\\\sin t&\cos t\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det DF_{(s,t)}=e^{2s}>0.
$$
Thus its <total derivative> is invertible everywhere, and the <inverse function theorem> makes it locally invertible. But $F(s,t+2\pi)=F(s,t)$, so it is not <injective> and has no global inverse on its image. The domain is open and <connected> as required.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**No.** On $\Omega=\mathbb R^2$ let
$$
\boxed{F(s,t)=(s^3,t).}
$$
It is a <bijection> onto $\mathbb R^2$, with inverse $(u,v)\mapsto(\sqrt[3]u,v)$. Nevertheless $DF_{(s,t)}=\operatorname{diag}(3s^2,1)$ is singular whenever $s=0$. Set-theoretic invertibility, even together with a continuous inverse, does not force invertibility of the <total derivative>. If the inverse were assumed differentiable, the <chain rule> applied to $F^{-1}\circ F$ would force both derivatives to be invertible; that stronger assumption is absent here.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Fix $x\in U$. Since $U$ is open, choose an open <ball> $B(x,\varepsilon)\subset U$. For any $y$ in this ball the segment $x+t(y-x)$ remains in it. For each coordinate $G_j$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\frac d{dt}G_j(x+t(y-x))=DG_j{}_{x+t(y-x)}(y-x)=0.
$$
The ordinary <mean value theorem> therefore gives $G_j(y)=G_j(x)$ for every coordinate, hence $G(y)=G(x)$. Thus $G$ is a <locally constant function>.

For each $z\in\mathbb R^m$, its <preimage> is a union of such open balls, so $G^{-1}(\{z\})$ is open in $\mathbb R^n$. Its complement in $U$ is the union of the other fibers and is also open. If $U$ is nonempty and <connected>, the nonempty fiber through any chosen point is both open and closed in $U$, and must be all of $U$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{DG\equiv0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad G\text{ locally constant, and constant on connected }U.}
$$
The empty-domain case is vacuous. Notice that continuity of $DG$ was never needed.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Write $a=\partial_1f_1=\partial_2f_2$ and $b=\partial_2f_1=-\partial_1f_2$. The <Cauchy-Riemann equations> give
$$
DF_x=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\-b&a\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det DF_x=a^2+b^2.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{DF_x\text{ is invertible exactly when }(\partial_1f_1(x),\partial_2f_1(x))\ne(0,0).}
$$
Equivalently, it is singular exactly when all four <partial derivatives> vanish. The sum of squares is over $\mathbb R$, so no cancellation is possible.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Suppose $DF_x$ were invertible at some $x\in\Omega$. The <inverse function theorem> supplies a neighborhood $V$ of $x$ for which $F(V)$ is a nonempty open subset of $\mathbb R^2$. But $F(V)\subset M$, and a one-dimensional <embedded submanifold> of $\mathbb R^2$ has empty interior. To see this last assertion, a <slice chart for an embedded submanifold> takes $M$ locally into a coordinate line; a coordinate line cannot contain an open plane neighborhood, and a chart preserves openness. This is a contradiction.

Thus $DF_x$ is singular everywhere. Part (c)(i) strengthens singularity to $DF_x=0$ everywhere. By (b) and <connectedness> of $\Omega$,
$$
\boxed{F\text{ is constant}.}
$$
The special form of the <Jacobian matrix> is essential: an arbitrary differentiable map can have nonconstant image in a curve with derivative of rank one.

