= 1
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

**Every $a\in\mathbb R$ works.** Translation preserves a <smooth function>, and the <support of a function> becomes the compact interval $[a-1,a+1]$. Hence $\phi(\,\cdot-a)\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R)$, the <space of test functions>.

= 2
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}
{c}

**Exactly $a\ne0$ works.** For nonzero $a$, composition with $x\mapsto ax$ preserves smoothness, and the <support of a function> is $[-1/|a|,1/|a|]$, which is compact. For $a=0$ the result is the strictly positive constant $\phi(0)$ on all of $\mathbb R$, so it lacks <compact support> and is not a <test function>.

= 3
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}
{c}

**Exactly $|a|>1$ works.** Composition with the <hyperbolic tangent> is smooth for every $a$. If $|a|>1$, set $R=\operatorname{arctanh}(1/|a|)$. For $|x|\ge R$, $|a\tanh x|\ge1$, so the result vanishes; it is therefore a <test function> with <support of a function> $[-R,R]$.

If $|a|\le1$, every finite $x$ satisfies $|a\tanh x|<1$. The given positivity makes $\phi(|a|\tanh x)>0$ for every $x$, so its support is all of $\mathbb R$. In particular $|a|=1$ is excluded even though the function tends to zero at infinity: decay is not <compact support>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Multiplication of a <distribution> by a <smooth function> is defined by moving that function onto the <test function>. The <Dirac delta multiplication identity> gives
$$
\boxed{\langle x^2\delta,\phi\rangle=\langle\delta,x^2\phi\rangle=(x^2\phi(x))\big|_{x=0}=0.}
$$
In fact $x^2\delta$ is the zero <distribution>, independently of the special positivity assumptions on $\phi$.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Write $F_j(p)=\mathcal Lf_j(p)$, using the square-root branch positive on real $p>0$. Integrating the differential equation and using boundedness at zero gives
$$
xf_1(x)=\int_0^x tf_0(t)\,dt.
$$
The integration constant is zero since $xf_1(x)\to0$. Continuity of $f_0$ then shows $f_1(x)=O(x)$, so $f_1$ extends continuously with $f_1(0)=0$.

The <Laplace transform of a derivative> and <differentiation under the integral sign> give
$$
-pF_1'(p)=-F_0'(p),\qquad F_1'(p)=\frac{F_0'(p)}p=-\frac1{(p^2+1)^{3/2}}.
$$
Integrating and using $F_1(p)\to0$ as real $p\to\infty$ fixes the constant:
$$
\boxed{F_1(p)=1-\frac p{\sqrt{p^2+1}}.}
$$
The <initial value theorem for Laplace transforms> gives $f_0(0)=\lim_{p\to\infty}pF_0(p)=1$. It is tempting to apply the derivative rule immediately to $f_0$, but its stated regularity is only continuity. To justify that step, define $h(x)=1-\int_0^x f_1(t)\,dt$. Then
$$
\mathcal Lh(p)=\frac{1-F_1(p)}p=\frac1{\sqrt{p^2+1}}=F_0(p).
$$
Uniqueness of the <Laplace transform> gives $h=f_0$, and hence $f_0$ is continuously differentiable and
$$
\boxed{f_1=-f_0'.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Use the causal <convolution> $(f_0*f_1)(x)=\int_0^x f_0(t)f_1(x-t)\,dt$. The <convolution theorem for Laplace transforms> and (i) give
$$
\mathcal L(f_0*f_1)(p)=F_0(p)F_1(p)=\frac1{\sqrt{p^2+1}}-\frac p{p^2+1}.
$$
Since $\mathcal L(\cos x)=p/(p^2+1)$, uniqueness of the <Laplace transform> yields
$$
\boxed{(f_0*f_1)(x)=f_0(x)-\cos x,\qquad x\ge0.}
$$
At $x=0$ the right side is $1-1=0$, agreeing with the empty <convolution> integral. Identification of $f_0$ as a <Bessel function> is unnecessary.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Use the <Fourier transform> convention $\widehat u(s,t)=\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-isx}u(x,t)\,dx$. Then $\widehat{u_{xx}}=-s^2\widehat u$ and $\widehat{xu}=i\partial_s\widehat u$, so
$$
\widehat{\partial_x(xu)}=is(i\partial_s\widehat u)=-s\partial_s\widehat u.
$$
Thus the transformed <partial differential equation> and <initial condition> are
$$
\boxed{\partial_t\widehat u+s\partial_s\widehat u=-s^2\widehat u,\qquad\widehat u(s,0)=1.}
$$
The last equality is the <Fourier transform> of the <Dirac delta distribution>. The two terms in $\partial_x(xu)=u+xu_x$ must be transformed together; dropping $u$ would give the wrong equation.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Substitute $\widehat u=e^{-b(t)s^2}$ into the transformed <partial differential equation>. Comparing coefficients gives $b'=1-2b$, $b(0)=0$, whence
$$
\boxed{b(t)=\frac{1-e^{-2t}}2,\qquad\widehat u(s,t)=\exp\!\left[-\frac{1-e^{-2t}}2s^2\right].}
$$
The stated Gaussian <Fourier transform> pair, with its inverse factor $1/(2\pi)$, gives
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi(1-e^{-2t})}}\exp\!\left[-\frac{x^2}{2(1-e^{-2t})}\right],\qquad t>0.}
$$
This is a <normal distribution> density with mean zero and <variance> $1-e^{-2t}$. Its total integral is one. As $t\downarrow0$ it converges to $\delta$ in the sense of <distributions>, while as $t\to\infty$ it tends to the standard <normal distribution> density. This is the transition density from zero of the <Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process> with drift $-x$ and diffusion coefficient $\sqrt2$.

= 6
{parent=2021}
{scope}
{title2=Calculus of variations}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Let $g>0$ be the gravitational acceleration, and let $y$ denote positive downward depth. For a frictionless particle released from rest, <conservation of energy> gives $v^2=2gy$. The <arc length> element of $(x,-y(x))$ is $ds=\sqrt{1+y'^2}\,dx$, so the <travel time> and length are
$$
T[y]=\frac1{\sqrt{2g}}\int_0^1\frac{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}{\sqrt y}\,dx,\qquad S[y]=\int_0^1\sqrt{1+y'^2}\,dx.
$$
For a finite-time single arch, require $y>0$ in $0<x<1$, $y(0)=y(1)=0$, and interpret the endpoint integrals improperly. At a regular constrained minimizer with $S[y]=L$, the <Lagrange multiplier> rule for an <isoperimetric constraint> makes $\sqrt{2g}\,T+\lambda(S-L)$ stationary. Dropping the constant $-\lambda L$ gives
$$
\boxed{I[y]=\int_0^1(y^{-1/2}+\lambda)\sqrt{1+y'^2}\,dx,\qquad y(0)=y(1)=0.}
$$
For $L>1$ the constraint is regular under interior <variations>: if its first variation vanished for every compactly supported variation, the <Euler-Lagrange equation> for $S$ would force $y'$ constant, hence $y\equiv0$ and $S=1$, a contradiction. Thus the multiplier is justified for a nondegenerate arch.

**The printed case $L=1$ is degenerate.** Since $\sqrt{1+y'^2}\ge1$, equality of the length with the horizontal separation forces $y'=0$ and $y=0$. A particle initially at rest then has no tangential gravitational acceleration and cannot complete the trip. Its <travel time> is infinite. The finite-time calculation therefore concerns $L>1$, with endpoint regularity qualified below.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Set $A(y)=y^{-1/2}+\lambda$ and $F(y,y')=A(y)\sqrt{1+y'^2}$. Since there is no explicit $x$ dependence, the <Beltrami identity> says $F-y'F_{y'}$ is constant along a stationary curve. One can verify it directly:
$$
\frac d{dx}(F-y'F_{y'})=y'\left(F_y-\frac d{dx}F_{y'}\right)=0
$$
by the <Euler-Lagrange equation>. Here $F_{y'}=A(y)y'/\sqrt{1+y'^2}$, so
$$
\boxed{H=F-y'F_{y'}=\frac{y^{-1/2}+\lambda}{\sqrt{1+y'^2}}\quad\text{is constant}.}
$$
This identity applies on the open arch where $y>0$. The constant is finite by evaluating at any interior point. Near an endpoint $A(y)>0$, hence $H>0$ everywhere on such an arch. Thus also $A(y)>0$ throughout it.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

With $H>0$, set
$$
s=\sin(\phi/2),\qquad y^{-1/2}+\lambda=\frac Hs,\qquad c=H^2>0,\qquad b=\frac\lambda H.
$$
Then the <first integral> gives $1/\sqrt{1+y'^2}=s$. On the descending and ascending halves of the physical track choose respectively $0<\phi<\pi$ and $\pi<\phi<2\pi$, so that $y'=\cot(\phi/2)$. Solving for $y$ gives
$$
\boxed{y(\phi)=\frac{\sin^2(\phi/2)}{c(1-b\sin(\phi/2))^2}.}
$$
The unsquared equation requires $1-bs>0$. At $s=1$ this says **$b<1$**; it is not permissible to retain other values merely because the displayed formula for $y$ has a squared denominator.

For completeness, the <Euler-Lagrange equation> also gives $y''=(A'(y)/A(y))(1+y'^2)<0$. Thus $y'$ decreases through zero exactly once, so the chosen parameter runs through the whole single arch without an extra branch. Differentiating the formula for $y$ and dividing by $y'=\cot(\phi/2)$ gives, continuously also at $\phi=\pi$,
$$
\frac{dx}{d\phi}=\frac{\sin^2(\phi/2)}{c(1-b\sin(\phi/2))^3}.
$$
Using $x(0)=0$ yields
$$
\boxed{x(\phi)=\frac1c\int_0^\phi\frac{\sin^2(u/2)}{(1-b\sin(u/2))^3}\,du.}
$$
Since $\sqrt{1+y'^2}=1/\sin(\phi/2)$, the <arc length> is obtained by cancelling one factor of $\sin(\phi/2)$ in $dx/d\phi$. The conditions $x(2\pi)=1$ and $S=L$ become
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
c&=\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{\sin^2(u/2)}{(1-b\sin(u/2))^3}\,du,\\
L&=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{\sin(u/2)}{(1-b\sin(u/2))^3}\,du}{\displaystyle\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{\sin^2(u/2)}{(1-b\sin(u/2))^3}\,du}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
These determine a unique parameter pair for each $L>1$. To see uniqueness, regard $L$ as the <expected value> of $1/s$ under a <probability density function> proportional to $s^2/(1-bs)^3$ on $0<u<2\pi$. <Differentiation under the integral sign> gives the <covariance> identity
$$
\frac{dL}{db}=3\operatorname{Cov}\!\left(\frac1s,\frac{s}{1-bs}\right)<0.
$$
The first argument decreases with $s$ and the second increases. The strict sign follows by applying $(f(s)-f(t))(g(s)-g(t))<0$ to two independent unequal values and averaging; the integrable endpoint singularity of $1/s$ causes no problem. As $b\uparrow1$, the weight concentrates at $s=1$, so $L\downarrow1$. As $b=-k\to-\infty$, endpoint estimates give numerator $\sim2/k^2$ and denominator $\sim4\log k/k^3$, so $L\sim k/(2\log k)\to\infty$. Continuity then covers every $L>1$. At $b=0$ one recovers the <cycloid> and $L=4/\pi$.

**Smoothness qualification.** Near $\phi=0$,
$$
x\sim\frac{\phi^3}{12c},\qquad y\sim\frac{\phi^2}{4c},\qquad y'\to+\infty,
$$
and the other endpoint has $y'\to-\infty$. These are smooth graphs on $0<x<1$, not $C^1$ graphs on the closed interval. Indeed a finite endpoint slope would make the conserved $H$ diverge as $y\to0$. The <travel time> remains finite because
$$
T=\frac1{\sqrt{2gc}}\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{d\phi}{(1-b\sin(\phi/2))^2}<\infty\qquad(b<1).
$$
Thus the printed phrase “smooth solution” must be understood on the interior, with improper endpoint limits. The variational calculation supplies necessary stationary arches; it does not by itself prove global minimality in every larger class of tracks.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

Put $f(\phi)=dx/d\phi$. Since $\sin((2\pi-\phi)/2)=\sin(\phi/2)$, both $f(2\pi-\phi)=f(\phi)$ and $y(2\pi-\phi)=y(\phi)$. Using $x(2\pi)=1$ in the integral for $x$ gives
$$
\boxed{x(2\pi-\phi)=1-x(\phi),\qquad y(2\pi-\phi)=y(\phi).}
$$
Hence the physical track $(x,-y)$ is symmetric about $x=1/2$, with $x(\pi)=1/2$. For $0<\phi<2\pi$, $dx/d\phi>0$, and
$$
\frac{dy}{d\phi}=\frac{\sin(\phi/2)\cos(\phi/2)}{c(1-b\sin(\phi/2))^3}
$$
is positive before $\pi$ and negative after $\pi$. Therefore the **physical height $-y$ has its unique minimum at $x=1/2$**, with
$$
\boxed{y_{\max}=\frac1{c(1-b)^2},\qquad (x,\text{height})_{\min}=\left(\frac12,-\frac1{c(1-b)^2}\right).}
$$
The depth $y$ has a maximum there. This proves the intended claim for every finite-time member $L>1$; $L=1$ has the degeneracy described in (a).

