{title=Stationary brachistochrone arches at several fixed lengths, with vertical endpoint tangents}

= 7
{parent=2021}
{scope}
{title2=Graph theory}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

For a finite <connected graph>, a <spanning tree> contains every <graph vertex> and is connected and acyclic. A <minimum spanning tree> minimizes $\sum_{e\in E(T)}w(e)$ over all such <trees>. A <shortest-path tree> rooted at $v$ is a <spanning tree> whose unique $v$-to-$u$ <graph path> has length $d_G(v,u)$ for every <graph vertex> $u$. Here the <weighted path length> is the sum of its <edge> weights. The two optimizations generally differ, as (b) shows. Connectedness is needed for either spanning-tree object to exist.

For the grid, <Kruskal's algorithm> examines <edges> in nondecreasing order of weight and accepts an <edge> exactly when it joins two different components of the current <forest>. The weights are encountered in the order
$$
1,\ \frac32,\ 2,\ \frac52,\ 3,\ \frac72,\ldots.
$$
The horizontal and vertical acceptance rules are detailed in the two numbered subanswers. Together they produce
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
E(T)={}&\{(x,y)(x+1,y):1\le x<n,\ 1\le y\le n,\ x\ge y-1\}\\
&\cup\{(x,y)(x,y+1):1\le x\le n,\ 1\le y<n,\ y\ge x\}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
There are $(n-1)(n+2)/2$ horizontal and $n(n-1)/2$ vertical <edges>, totaling $n^2-1$. Every accepted <edge> attaches a previously isolated <graph vertex> to the component already built, so the result is connected and acyclic. The correctness theorem for <Kruskal's algorithm>, which follows from the <minimum spanning tree cut property>, proves minimality. Its total cost is
$$
\boxed{w(T)=\left[\frac{n(n+1)(n+2)}6-1\right]+\frac{n(n-1)(2n+5)}{12}=\frac{(n-1)(4n^2+13n+12)}{12}.}
$$
For $n=1$ the <tree> has one <graph vertex>, no <edges> and cost zero. The source's numbered items specify the two <edge> types; the following subanswers record their contributions to the algorithm.

\Image[2021-grid-spanning-tree.png]
{title=Kruskal spanning tree of the weighted grid, with accepted horizontal and vertical edges highlighted}

= 1
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

At weight $y$ the <edges> considered are precisely the horizontal <edges> in row $y$. For $y=1$ all $n$ <graph vertices> in that row are isolated, so all $n-1$ <edges> are accepted. For $y\ge2$, the first $y-1$ <graph vertices> in that row already belong to the one component built by earlier stages, because vertical columns $1,\ldots,y-1$ have already been processed. The other <graph vertices> are isolated. Thus the <edges> with $x<y-1$ would create a <graph cycle>, and all the <edges> with $x\ge y-1$ are accepted, successively attaching the remaining <graph vertices>. The rule is
$$
\boxed{(x,y)(x+1,y)\text{ is accepted exactly when }x\ge y-1.}
$$
The selected <edges> of equal weight have no cycles after the existing component is contracted, so tie ordering does not affect this conclusion. Their combined contribution to the <minimum spanning tree> cost is
$$
(n-1)+\sum_{y=2}^n y(n-y+1)=\frac{n(n+1)(n+2)}6-1.
$$

= 2
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}
{c}

At weight $x+1/2$, column $x$ is considered just after horizontal row $x$. At this point its first $x$ <graph vertices> already lie in the component built so far, while <graph vertices> above them in that column are isolated. Consequently vertical <edges> with $y<x$ create a <graph cycle>, while those with $y\ge x$ attach the isolated <graph vertices> in a chain:
$$
\boxed{(x,y)(x,y+1)\text{ is accepted exactly when }y\ge x.}
$$
Together with the horizontal rule this gives an <induction> on the alternating row and column stages and justifies the component description used in both numbered answers. The vertical contribution to the <minimum spanning tree> cost is
$$
\sum_{x=1}^{n-1}\left(x+\frac12\right)(n-x)=\frac{n(n-1)(2n+5)}{12}.
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Use a triangle with <graph vertices> $v,a,b$ and <edge> weights
$$
w(va)=2,\qquad w(ab)=3,\qquad w(vb)=4.
$$
Its unique <minimum spanning tree> is $\{va,ab\}$, of cost $5$, because the other two spanning-tree costs are $6,7$. In this <tree> the $v$-to-$b$ <graph path> has length $2+3=5$, whereas the <edge> $vb$ gives length $4$ in the original <graph>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{T_{\mathrm{MST}}=\{va,ab\}\ne T_{\mathrm{shortest}}=\{va,vb\}.}
$$
Since the <minimum spanning tree> is unique, none is a <shortest-path tree> rooted at $v$.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Assume throughout (c) that the <graph> is finite and connected, as needed for the asserted spanning <trees>. List its <edge> weights as $w_1<\cdots<w_m$. The stated separation implies $w_{j+1}>2w_j$, so for every $k$,
$$
\sum_{j<k}w_j<w_k\sum_{r=1}^{k-1}2^{-r}<w_k.
$$
Thus the weights form a <superincreasing sequence>. If $A,B$ are distinct <edge> sets, let $e$ be the heaviest <edge> in their <symmetric difference>. Suppose $e\in A$. All other uncancelled <edges> are lighter, so
$$
\sum_{a\in A}w(a)-\sum_{b\in B}w(b)\ge w(e)-\sum_{w(f)<w(e)}w(f)>0.
$$
Therefore different <edge> sets have different total costs. In particular all distinct <spanning trees> have different costs. Since a finite <connected graph> has at least one <spanning tree> and only finitely many choices,
$$
\boxed{\text{the minimum spanning tree exists and is unique}.}
$$
This domination of every collection of smaller <edges> will also be used in (iii).

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By (i), two simple <graph paths> with different <edge> sets have different lengths. In particular two distinct simple <graph paths> from the fixed root $v$ to the same <graph vertex> $u$ have different <edge> sets, so there is a **unique shortest <graph path> from $v$ to each $u$**. Positive weights ensure that a shortest walk has no repeated <graph vertex>: removing a <graph cycle> strictly reduces its length.

For each $u\ne v$, take the final <edge> of its unique shortest <graph path>. Every initial segment of a shortest <graph path> is itself shortest, since replacing it by a shorter one would shorten the whole route. Thus these <edges> agree with the <graph paths>' unique predecessors. Following a predecessor strictly decreases $d_G(v,u)$, so no predecessor <graph cycle> is possible, and all <graph vertices> eventually connect to $v$. They form a <shortest-path tree>. Every other such <tree> would have to use the same unique root-to-vertex <graph paths>, proving
$$
\boxed{\text{the shortest-path tree rooted at }v\text{ is unique}.}
$$
**Literal wording qualification.** “Any two paths” needs a convention. Reversing an undirected <graph path> preserves its <edge> set and length; distinct zero-edge <graph paths> at different <graph vertices> also have length zero. Thus the unrestricted statement is false if these count as different <graph paths>. The proved edge-set assertion, and in particular uniqueness for <graph paths> with the same ordered endpoints, is exactly what is needed for the deduction. Arbitrary walks are not included.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Let $T$ be the unique <minimum spanning tree>. If $e=ab$ is an <edge> outside $T$, adding it creates a unique <graph cycle> consisting of $e$ and the <tree> <graph path> $P_T(a,b)$. Every <edge> $f$ on that <graph path> has $w(f)<w(e)$: otherwise the distinct weights would give $w(f)>w(e)$, and replacing $f$ by $e$ would produce a cheaper <spanning tree>. This is the <minimum spanning tree cycle property> in exchange form.

By the domination inequality in (i), it follows more strongly that
$$
\boxed{w(P_T(a,b))<w(e).}
$$
Now consider any simple <graph path> $P$ from $v$ to $u$ that is not the <tree> <graph path>. It must use an <edge> outside $T$. Replace every such <edge> by the corresponding <tree> <graph path>. Each replacement strictly reduces length and produces a walk entirely in $T$. Removing any repeated-vertex excursions leaves the unique <tree> <graph path> $P_T(v,u)$ and can only reduce length further. Therefore
$$
w(P_T(v,u))<w(P).
$$
So every root-to-vertex <graph path> in $T$ is the unique shortest <graph path> in $G$, and
$$
\boxed{T_{\mathrm{MST}}=T_{\mathrm{shortest},v}.}
$$
The argument works for every choice of root $v$.

= 8
{parent=2021}
{scope}
{title2=Special relativity}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Differentiate the <worldline> with respect to $\theta$:
$$
\frac{dt}{d\theta}=\sqrt2T\cos\theta>0,\qquad \frac{dx}{d\theta}=-\sqrt2cT\kappa\sin\theta,\qquad\frac1c\frac{dx}{dt}=-\kappa\tan\theta.
$$
Since $|\tan\theta|\le1$ and $0<\kappa<1$, this is a future-directed <timelike curve>. The <proper time> element satisfies $c^2d\tau^2=c^2dt^2-dx^2$, hence
$$
\frac{d\tau}{d\theta}=\sqrt2T\sqrt{\cos^2\theta-\kappa^2\sin^2\theta}.
$$
Alice's total <proper time> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\tau_A=2\sqrt2T\int_0^{\pi/4}\sqrt{1-(1+\kappa^2)\sin^2\theta}\,d\theta.}
$$
This definite integral is an exact answer; it is an <elliptic integral of the second kind>. In notation $E(\alpha\mid m)=\int_0^\alpha\sqrt{1-m\sin^2u}\,du$, it is $2\sqrt2T E(\pi/4\mid1+\kappa^2)$. Although this parameter exceeds one, the integrand is real on the stated interval because its minimum squared value is $(1-\kappa^2)/2>0$.

The endpoint events have $(t,x)=(0,0)$ and $(2T,0)$. Sam stays at $x=0$ in his <inertial frame>, so his elapsed <proper time> is $2T$. For $\kappa>0$, the integrand is strictly less than $\cos\theta$ except at $\theta=0$, and $\tau_A<2T$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

On the fixed parameter interval the integrand tends to $\cos\theta$ as $\kappa\to0$ and is bounded by $\cos\theta$. The <dominated convergence theorem>, or <uniform convergence> here, gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{\kappa\to0}\tau_A=2\sqrt2T\int_0^{\pi/4}\cos\theta\,d\theta=2T.}
$$
This equals Sam's time between departure and reunion. Physically, $dx/dt=-c\kappa\tan\theta$ tends uniformly to zero, so Alice's <worldline> approaches Sam's, and the <time dilation> disappears.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

In the common $ct,x_1$ plane, multiplication of the two <Lorentz boost> matrices gives
$$
\begin{pmatrix}\cosh\theta&\sinh\theta\\\sinh\theta&\cosh\theta\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\cosh\theta'&\sinh\theta'\\\sinh\theta'&\cosh\theta'\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}\cosh(\theta+\theta')&\sinh(\theta+\theta')\\\sinh(\theta+\theta')&\cosh(\theta+\theta')\end{pmatrix}.
$$
This follows from the addition identities for <hyperbolic sine> and <hyperbolic cosine>. The transverse coordinates are unchanged by both transformations. Consequently the <rapidity> of $\overline O$ relative to $O$ is
$$
\boxed{\theta''=\theta+\theta'.}
$$
The signed rapidities add because both boosts use the same spatial axis and the stated matrix convention.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By definition $v/c=\tanh\theta$ and $v'/c=\tanh\theta'$. Using the addition identity for the <hyperbolic tangent> and the <rapidity> sum from (i),
$$
\frac{v''}c=\tanh(\theta+\theta')=\frac{v/c+v'/c}{1+vv'/c^2}.
$$
Thus the <velocity-addition formula> is
$$
\boxed{v''=\frac{v+v'}{1+vv'/c^2}=\frac{c^2(v+v')}{c^2+vv'}.}
$$
The denominator is positive for the permitted subluminal signed velocities. For the nonnegative speeds in (iii), the composite is also nonnegative and remains below $c$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

When both speeds are much smaller than $c$, expand the denominator of the <velocity-addition formula>:
$$
v''=(v+v')\left[1-\frac{vv'}{c^2}+O\!\left(\frac{v^2v'^2}{c^4}\right)\right],\qquad\boxed{v''\simeq v+v'.}
$$
This is the <Galilean transformation> limit. For $0\le\beta=v/c<1$ and $0\le\beta'=v'/c<1$,
$$
1-\frac{v''}c=\frac{(1-\beta)(1-\beta')}{1+\beta\beta'}>0.
$$
If both $v,v'\to c$ from below, the right side tends to zero, so
$$
\boxed{v''\to c\text{ from below}.}
$$
No sum of these subluminal speeds exceeds the <speed of light>.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Let $V>0$ be the observer's speed, so its laboratory velocity is $-V\mathbf e_x$. The particle's laboratory speed along $+y$ follows from the <energy-momentum relation>:
$$
u=c\sqrt{1-\frac{m^2c^4}{E^2}},\qquad q=\frac uc\in(0,1).
$$
Here $E>mc^2$ because the massive particle is moving. Boosting to the observer's frame with velocity $-V$ gives, by the <relativistic velocity-addition formula>,
$$
u'_x=V,\qquad u'_y=\frac u\gamma,\qquad \gamma=(1-V^2/c^2)^{-1/2}.
$$
Equivalently its <four-momentum> has $p'_x=\gamma VE/c^2$, $p'_y=p_y$. Thus $\tan\phi=u/(\gamma V)$. Solving this equation, rather than using the Galilean transverse velocity, yields
$$
\boxed{V=\frac{u}{\sqrt{\tan^2\phi+u^2/c^2}}=\frac{c q\cos\phi}{\sqrt{\sin^2\phi+q^2\cos^2\phi}}.}
$$
For a strictly moving observer, $0<\phi<\pi/2$. At $\phi=\pi/2$ the limiting observer speed is zero; as $\phi\downarrow0$ it tends to $c$. The direction of the observer's velocity remains negative $x$; the displayed $V$ is its positive magnitude.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For a <photon>, $m=0$ and $u=c$, so the result from (i) reduces to
$$
\boxed{V=c\cos\phi,\qquad \boldsymbol v_O=-c\cos\phi\,\mathbf e_x.}
$$
This is <relativistic aberration> for a photon initially transverse to the boost. It also follows directly from $p'_x=\gamma VE/c^2$ and $E'=\gamma E$: the observed direction cosine is $cp'_x/E'=V/c$. The transverse observed speed is $c/\gamma=c\sin\phi$, and the total photon speed is still $c$. If desired the observed photon energy is $E'=E/\sin\phi$ for $0<\phi\le\pi/2$.

= 9
{parent=2021}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical modelling in biology}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The per-capita net growth rate is $r/(1+bN^2)-\delta$. Thus **$r$ is the low-density per-capita birth or recruitment rate**, **$\delta$ is the constant per-capita death or removal rate**, and **$b$ controls density-dependent suppression of recruitment**. Both $r$ and $\delta$ have units of inverse time; $b$ has units of inverse population squared. The population scale $b^{-1/2}$ halves the recruitment rate from its low-density value. Larger $b$ makes suppression important at smaller population sizes. These are interpretations of this phenomenological <population dynamics> model, rather than independently specified biological mechanisms.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Write $f(N)=N[r/(1+bN^2)-\delta]$. A <steady state> is a nonnegative zero of $f$. If $r<\delta$, the bracket is strictly negative for every $N\ge0$, so only $N=0$ exists. If $r>\delta$, there are exactly two nonnegative <steady states>,
$$
\boxed{N=0,\qquad N^*=\sqrt{\frac{r-\delta}{b\delta}}.}
$$
For an autonomous scalar <ordinary differential equation>, a <steady state> with $f'<0$ is locally asymptotically stable, while one with $f'>0$ is unstable. Here
$$
f'(N)=\frac{r(1-bN^2)}{(1+bN^2)^2}-\delta,\qquad f'(0)=r-\delta.
$$
At the positive <steady state>, use $1+b(N^*)^2=r/\delta$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{f'(N^*)=-\frac{2\delta(r-\delta)}r<0.}
$$
Therefore zero is **stable for $r<\delta$ and unstable for $r>\delta$**, whereas the positive <steady state> is **stable whenever it exists**. At the omitted equality $r=\delta$, $f(N)=-\delta bN^3/(1+bN^2)$; zero is nonhyperbolic but still attracts every nonnegative initial state.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Linearizing at the positive <steady state> gives $n'=f'(N^*)n$, so a small displacement decays as $n(t)=n(0)e^{-t/\tau}$. The <recovery time of a stable equilibrium> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\tau=-\frac1{f'(N^*)}=\frac r{2\delta(r-\delta)}.}
$$
As $\delta\uparrow r$ with $r,b$ fixed, $N^*\to0$ and $\tau\to\infty$. Thus the equilibrium population approaches extinction and recovers increasingly slowly after small perturbations. This is <critical slowing down>. Exactly at the threshold the leading equation is $N'\sim-rbN^3$, which gives algebraic decay $N(t)\sim(2rb t)^{-1/2}$ instead of exponential recovery.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

For $r>\delta$, the sign of $f(N)$ is positive on $0<N<N^*$ and negative on $N>N^*$. Its graph passes through $0,N^*$, has positive initial slope $r-\delta$, and eventually behaves like $-\delta N$. The <phase line> therefore points toward $N^*$ from both sides.

The vector field is continuously differentiable, so solutions are unique. No positive solution crosses either equilibrium; one starting below $N^*$ increases and stays below it, and one starting above it decreases and stays above it. These bounded solutions exist for all forward time and have a limit. By continuity of $f$, any such limit must be a zero of $f$: otherwise the derivative would stay bounded away from zero near the limit. The increasing solution is bounded below by its strictly positive initial value, excluding zero; the decreasing one stays above $N^*$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{N(0)>0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad N(t)\longrightarrow N^*.}
$$
The solution starting exactly at $N^*$ is constant. The zero initial condition remains at zero, which is why positivity is stipulated.

\Image[2021-population-bifurcation.png]
{title=Population growth curve and nonnegative steady-state bifurcation diagram}

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}
{c}

The nonnegative <bifurcation diagram> has the branch $N=0$ for every $r>0$, stable for $r<\delta$ and unstable for $r>\delta$. At $r=\delta$ the stable positive branch emerges:
$$
\boxed{N^*(r)=\sqrt{\frac{r-\delta}{b\delta}},\qquad r>\delta.}
$$
The figure in (iv) uses solid curves for stable <steady states> and a dashed curve for the unstable zero branch. At the meeting point the zero <steady state> is nonhyperbolic and attracts from the nonnegative side. If the equation is formally extended to negative $N$, there is also the symmetric branch $-N^*$, giving a <supercritical pitchfork bifurcation>; its negative half is omitted in the biological diagram. This qualification avoids calling the single nonnegative square-root branch a full pitchfork by itself.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

At a constant <steady state> $N_t=N$, the discrete equation reduces to
$$
N\left[\frac r{1+bN^2}-(\delta+1)\right]=0.
$$
Write $s=\delta+1>1$. For $r<s$, no nonzero real root is possible, so **zero is the only real steady state**. For $r>s$ the real roots are
$$
\boxed{N=0,\qquad N=\pm\sqrt{\frac{r-s}{bs}}.}
$$
Thus there is **exactly one strictly positive steady state**, $N^*=\sqrt{(r-s)/(bs)}$. There are three real steady states in total, two of them nonnegative. At $r=s$ the only one is zero. This distinction keeps the positive-population statement separate from the count over all real numbers.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $f(X,Y)=X[r/(1+bY^2)-\delta]$. At the positive <steady state>, $1+b(N^*)^2=r/s$, where $s=\delta+1$. The two <partial derivatives> are
$$
f_X(N^*,N^*)=1,\qquad f_Y(N^*,N^*)=-\frac{2rb(N^*)^2}{(1+b(N^*)^2)^2}=-\frac{2s(r-s)}r.
$$
The first-order <Taylor expansion> with $N_t=N^*+n_t$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{n_{t+1}-n_t+a n_{t-1}=0,\qquad a=\frac{2s(r-s)}r=\frac{2(r-(\delta+1))(\delta+1)}r.}
$$
Equivalently, the <linearization> on $(n_t,n_{t-1})$ has matrix $\begin{pmatrix}1&-a\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$ and <characteristic polynomial> $\mu^2-\mu+a$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

For $r>s$, $a>0$. When $0<a\le1/4$, the roots of $\mu^2-\mu+a$ are real and lie strictly between zero and one. When $a>1/4$, they are a complex-conjugate pair of modulus $\sqrt a$. Thus the positive <steady state> is linearly asymptotically stable exactly when $0<a<1$, and unstable when $a>1$. Since $a=2s-2s^2/r$ increases strictly with $r$, the crossing occurs at
$$
\boxed{a=1,\qquad r_c=\frac{2s^2}{2s-1}=\frac{2(\delta+1)^2}{2\delta+1}.}
$$
At the crossing,
$$
\mu_\pm=\frac{1\pm i\sqrt3}{2}=e^{\pm i\pi/3},\qquad \boxed{n_t=A\cos(\pi t/3)+B\sin(\pi t/3).}
$$
Every nonzero real linearized perturbation has period six. This establishes the intended **linear stability threshold and linearized period six**.

**The nonlinear period-six claim in the printed paper does not follow, and is false as a local bifurcating-branch claim for this map.** One can check this directly rather than infer a nonlinear period from the <eigenvalues>. At $r=r_c$ normalize $N_t=N^*(1+u)$, $N_{t-1}=N^*(1+v)$. The exact planar map is
$$
F(u,v)=\left((1+u)\left[\frac{2s^2}{2s-1+(1+v)^2}-(s-1)\right]-1,\ u\right).
$$
Its cubic <Taylor expansion> is
$$
F(u,v)=\left(u-v-uv+\frac{2-s}{2s}v^2+\frac{2-s}{2s}uv^2+\frac{s-1}{s^2}v^3,\ u\right)+O(\|(u,v)\|^4).
$$
Here is a concrete check of the sixfold return. Put $z=(u,v)^T$, $Q=u^2-uv+v^2$, and
$$
K=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\2&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad K^2=-3I,\qquad \alpha=-\frac{3s^2-3s+1}{s^2},\qquad\beta=-\frac{5s^2-6s+2}{2s^2}.
$$
Composing the displayed cubic expansion six times cancels all quadratic terms and gives
$$
F^6(z)-z=Q(\alpha I+\beta K)z+O(\|z\|^4).
$$
In particular
$$
\boxed{Q(F^6(z))-Q(z)=-\frac{2(3s^2-3s+1)}{s^2}Q(z)^2+O(\|z\|^5).}
$$
The quartic coefficient is strictly negative. Hence, sufficiently near the equilibrium, every nonzero point has smaller $Q$ after six iterations: the critical nonlinear map has no nearby nontrivial period-six orbit, and the equilibrium itself is locally attracting despite its neutral <linearization>.

One can also rule out a branch of small period-six orbits on either side of the crossing. Normalize about the positive equilibrium at each nearby $r$, and put $\eta=a-1$. The derivative of the sixth power of $\begin{pmatrix}1&-a\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$ at $a=1$ is $3I+K$. Smooth dependence on $r$ therefore gives
$$
F_\eta^6(z)-z=\big[(3\eta+\alpha Q)I+(\eta+\beta Q)K\big]z+o\big((|\eta|+\|z\|^2)\|z\|\big).
$$
The leading matrix has <determinant> $(3\eta+\alpha Q)^2+3(\eta+\beta Q)^2$. Both terms could vanish with $Q>0$ only if $\alpha=3\beta$, but
$$
\alpha-3\beta=\frac{(3s-2)^2}{2s^2}>0.
$$
Thus its norm on nonzero $z$ is bounded below by a positive constant times $(|\eta|+\|z\|^2)\|z\|$, and the smaller remainder cannot cancel it. There is no nearby nonzero solution of $F_\eta^6(z)=z$. The <eigenvalues> satisfy the nonresonance conditions through order four, their moduli cross outward with increasing $r$, and the cubic radial coefficient is negative. The standard <Neimark–Sacker bifurcation> theorem therefore gives a supercritical invariant closed curve. Its rotation is near the linear resonance $1/6$; it is not a local branch of exact period-six orbits.

Finally, the discrete population map does not preserve nonnegativity for arbitrary large lagged populations: $r/(1+bN_{t-1}^2)-\delta$ can be negative. The positive-equilibrium stability conclusions above are local and do not assume a global positivity property that the formula lacks.
